/// \defgroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc Packet checksum, GameSpy Peer helpers, the ping service and lockstep timeouts
/// \ingroup grp_network
/// \brief The rotate-and-add checksum behind packet, map and game-state
///        CRCs, three small functions of the GameSpy Peer SDK, the ping
///        service's interface, a disconnect-timeout stamp and the
///        connection queue-empty test.
///
/// These pieces sit on different seams of the network subsystem
/// (\ref sub_network):
/// - **Checksums.** The rotate-and-add checksum in
///   GameNetwork/native_packet_crc.cpp is the checksum routine that
///   BFME 2's network code, its LAN lobby, its quick-match request, its
///   firewall probe and the logic random-number state all call. Zero Hour
///   computes the same value inline with its CRC class.
/// - **GameSpy Peer SDK.** PeerOperationsComplete tells the SDK's blocking
///   calls when an operation has left the queue, peerStopGame marks the
///   local game stopped and then either updates or ends the hosted game's
///   server reporting, and peerParseQuery hands a received
///   server-browser query to the Query and Reporting 2 (QR2) parser.
/// - **Ping service.** PingerInterface is the interface of ThePinger, the
///   pool of worker threads that measure ICMP round-trip times.
/// - **Lockstep housekeeping.** DisconnectManager::resetPlayerTimeout
///   restarts one remote player's timeout, and Connection::isQueueEmpty
///   tells ConnectionManager when a connection has nothing left to send.
///
/// \par Modding notes
/// - None of these functions reads INI, map scripts, Lua or string tables.
///   The checksum algorithm, the GameSpy wire handling and the number of
///   ping workers are compiled in.
/// - The length of a player timeout is data: in Zero Hour it is the
///   GameData INI value NetworkPlayerTimeoutTime, read through
///   TheGlobalData. BFME 2's GameData field table also lists
///   NetworkPlayerTimeoutTime; which field BFME 2's timeout test reads is
///   inferred from offsets and is not settled here.
/// - Every machine in a game depends on the rotate-and-add checksum
///   producing the same value for the same bytes. Changing it breaks
///   packet acceptance against unmodified clients and LAN map checks, and
///   changes the game-state CRC that GameLogic::getCRC computes (see
///   GameNetwork/native_packet_crc.cpp). In Zero Hour that CRC is compared
///   between players and, during replay playback, against the recorded
///   values.
///
/// \par Remastering notes
/// - Transport, the GameSpy Peer SDK and the ping workers are Winsock code;
///   the ping workers also use ICMP.DLL (see MeasureIcmpPing). Timeouts and
///   quit times use winmm's timeGetTime, a millisecond wall clock, not the
///   logic frame counter.
/// - The checksum works on bytes, but some callers pass in-memory integers
///   (the logic random state, for example), so the result depends on the
///   little-endian layout of x86.
///
/// \see \ref sub_network, \ref grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping

/// \class PingerInterface
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief The ping service: a queue of ping requests served by worker
///        threads, and a queue of averaged results.
///
/// PingerInterface is an abstract interface. In Zero Hour its one
/// implementation, Pinger, is private to PingThread.cpp; BFME 2's Pinger
/// members follow the same order of virtual functions. The single
/// instance is the global ThePinger. In both Zero Hour and BFME 2,
/// SetUpGameSpy creates it after the ladder list and calls startThreads on
/// it, and TearDownGameSpy calls endThreads and deletes it, so the service
/// exists only while the online subsystem is set up.
///
/// A request names a host (a dotted address or a host name), a number of
/// repetitions and a timeout in milliseconds. A response carries the host,
/// the average round-trip time of the replies that arrived (or -1 when
/// none did) and the number of successful repetitions. In Zero Hour the
/// login screen and the DisconnectManager post requests, and the login
/// screen turns the results into the player's ping string with
/// getPingString. BFME 2's DisconnectManager::update also posts ping
/// requests to ThePinger, with a fixed repetition count and timeout, and
/// reads back every waiting response with getResponse.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - BFME 2's Pinger::startThreads first stops any running workers, then
///   starts ten PingThreadClass workers, the same count as Zero Hour.
///   BFME 2's startThreads also takes a lock before starting them, and
///   each worker keeps running only while a mutex it tries on every pass
///   of its loop stays held elsewhere; Zero Hour's workers test a flag
///   instead.
/// - Each worker (PingThreadClass::Thread_Function) takes one request with
///   getRequest, resolves the host (inet_addr when the text starts with a
///   digit, otherwise gethostbyname), pings it once per repetition through
///   MeasureIcmpPing and posts the average with addResponse.
/// - As in Zero Hour, the request queue, the response queue and the
///   per-host result map each have their own mutex, so the interface may
///   be called from the game thread while the workers run.
///
/// \warning A worker dereferences the result of gethostbyname without a
///          usable null check. Zero Hour's source tests the result for
///          null, but only sets a value that the next line overwrites,
///          and dereferences the result anyway; BFME 2's shipped body has
///          no test at all. In both, a host name that does not resolve
///          makes the worker read through a null pointer.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The workers are Win32 threads that call Winsock and ICMP.DLL directly.
/// A port needs a thread primitive, a mutex and an ICMP echo (or another
/// latency probe), and should add the missing lookup check.
///
/// \see MeasureIcmpPing, SetUpGameSpy, TearDownGameSpy

/// \class DisconnectManager
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief Watches the other players of a lockstep game and runs the
///        disconnect screen and the vote to drop a player who stops
///        responding.
///
/// In Zero Hour, ConnectionManager creates one DisconnectManager for a
/// game and attaches it to the disconnect menu; ConnectionManager passes
/// itself to most of its members. The manager keeps, among other state, a
/// time stamp per remote player, the disconnect votes and the disconnect
/// frame each player reported (\ref sub_network).
///
/// Both versions bring up the disconnect screen from the GameData time
/// NetworkDisconnectTime, but measure it differently. In Zero Hour,
/// DisconnectManager::update turns the screen on when the logic frame has
/// not advanced within that time. BFME 2's DisconnectManager::update tests
/// each player slot instead, and a slot counts as stalled only when all
/// three of these hold:
/// - the player is connected;
/// - the connection manager does not report the slot as active. Its test
///   accepts a connected player whose state, a per-player value the
///   connection manager keeps, is 1, 2 or 3; what those states mean is not
///   established;
/// - a timeout test fails. It fails when the wall-clock time since a stamp
///   stored with that player's connection passes the GameData time that
///   BFME 2's GameData field table names NetworkDisconnectTime. It never
///   fails for the local slot or a slot with no connection, and when the
///   stamp is unset the test stores the current time and passes. While a
///   GameLogic value is below a global threshold the test allows four
///   times that time. If that value is the logic frame counter, as some of
///   the project's sources read it, this is a longer allowance at the
///   start of a game; neither the value nor the threshold is established.
///
/// update counts the stalled players. For each player slot it also keeps a
/// stall count and a flag that marks the count as already charged; Zero
/// Hour's DisconnectManager has neither. On every update each stalled
/// player is charged to a count: its own, or the local player's when the
/// manager has sent pings and fewer than a tenth of their repetitions were
/// answered. Charging a count adds one to it only when its flag is clear,
/// and sets the flag. A remote player's flag clears on the first update in
/// which that player is not stalled, so its own count goes up at most once
/// per stall. The local player's flag clears only on an update in which no
/// player is stalled, so stalls charged to the local count while some
/// player stays stalled add one between them, however many players stall
/// or recover in that time. Because the count is chosen again on every
/// update, one stall can add one to both counts when the ping ratio
/// crosses a tenth while the stall lasts.
///
/// update considers turning the screen on only while at least one player
/// counts as stalled, the screen is off and no load screen is up (a
/// GameLogic field that the source reads as the load screen). It then
/// turns the screen on unless the local player's stall count has reached
/// five or fewer than two players remain; in
/// those cases it calls an address-named routine of TheNetwork instead.
/// In one game mode (a GameLogic mode value the source does not name) it
/// also asks a global object, which the source reads as the victory
/// conditions without that reading being established, whether the game is
/// decided, and if so takes the same TheNetwork path instead of turning
/// the screen on. The per-player timeout stamps are a separate
/// mechanism: DisconnectManager::hasPlayerTimedOut and
/// DisconnectManager::updateDisconnectStatus read them, update calls
/// updateDisconnectStatus only while the screen is on, and update resets
/// the stamp of each other player it does not count as stalled.
///
/// The per-player timeout stamps have one entry for each player except
/// the local one. Members convert a player slot to that index by removing
/// the local slot (Zero Hour: translatedSlotPosition): slots below the
/// local one keep their number, slots above it move down by one, and the
/// local slot has no entry. Other per-player tables use the raw slot:
/// Zero Hour's vote and disconnect-frame tables, for example, and BFME 2's
/// stall counts.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The timers run on each machine's wall clock, not on logic frames. In
/// BFME 2 the stall test that brings up the disconnect screen reads the
/// GameData value NetworkDisconnectTime (or four times it under the
/// threshold described above), so changing that INI value changes how soon
/// the screen appears. In Zero Hour all of the disconnect timeouts are GameData INI
/// values (NetworkDisconnectTime, NetworkPlayerTimeoutTime and
/// NetworkDisconnectScreenNotifyTime).
///
/// \see DisconnectManager::resetPlayerTimeout, Connection,
///      ConnectionManager::update

/// \class Connection
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief The lockstep layer's link to one other player: the queue of
///        commands still to be sent to that player or acknowledged by it.
///
/// ConnectionManager keeps one Connection for each other player. In Zero
/// Hour a connection owns a NetCommandList of outgoing commands.
/// Connection::sendNetCommandMsg adds to it, Connection::doSend packs
/// commands into packets for the connection's Transport and drops those
/// that need no acknowledgement, and Connection::processAck removes a
/// command once the other player acknowledges it. The connection also
/// keeps retry timing and latency statistics.
///
/// \par Lifetime
/// When a player leaves, Connection::setQuitting stores a quit frame and
/// the time. BFME 2's ConnectionManager::update keeps sending on each
/// connection and deletes one only when its quit frame is not later than
/// the current logic frame and Connection::isQueueEmpty reports that its
/// queue has drained. Commands already queued for a leaving player keep
/// being sent until the queue is empty, or until Connection::doSend gives
/// up after a fixed flush window measured from the stored quit time and
/// discards what is left. Zero Hour's doSend has the same window.
///
/// \see Connection::isQueueEmpty, Connection::setQuitting,
///      ConnectionManager::areAllQueuesEmpty, ConnectionManager::update

/// \fn int PeerOperationsComplete(PeerState *peer, void *owner)
/// \brief Reports whether the GameSpy peer connection has no queued
///        operation with the given operation ID.
///
/// The GameSpy Peer SDK runs requests such as connecting, joining a room
/// or listing group rooms as queued operations, each with an ID. When a
/// request is made in blocking mode, its function loops, sleeping and
/// letting the SDK think, until this returns true and the operation's
/// callback has also been delivered. Its callers in the game executable
/// include the SDK's request functions, for example peerConnectA,
/// peerJoinTitleRoomA, peerChangeNickA and peerListGroupRoomsA, which call
/// it in that wait loop.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A connection without an operation list counts as finished. Otherwise
/// the list (a GameSpy DArray of operation pointers) is scanned, and the
/// result is false as soon as one operation's ID equals the argument.
///
/// \param peer  The peer connection (the SDK's PEER handle).
/// \param owner The operation ID to look for. It is compared with the field
///              that the tree's peerOperations.c names the operation's ID,
///              and every caller passes an operation ID; the name owner is
///              the donor's reading of that field.
/// \return 1 when no queued operation has that ID (or there is no list),
///         0 while one is still queued.
///
/// \see peerStopGame, peerParseQuery

/// \fn void peerStopGame(PeerGameConnection *peer)
/// \brief GameSpy Peer SDK: tells the SDK that the local game has stopped,
///        then updates or ends the game's server reporting.
///
/// The GameSpy server list learns about a hosted game through query
/// reporting. In Zero Hour, PeerThreadClass::stopHostingAlready calls this
/// before closing the reporting socket, and the peer thread also calls it
/// when the player joins a group room or leaves a staging room it was
/// hosting. In BFME 2, PeerThreadClass::stopHostingAlready reaches it
/// through a small helper.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Clears one state field of the connection and calls piSetLocalFlags,
///   the SDK's update of the local player's flags.
/// - If the connection holds a query-reporting record (the first of the
///   two records that peerParseQuery uses), it calls piSendStateChanged
///   when a second connection field is set and piStopReporting otherwise.
///   Which state that second field records is not established from BFME 2
///   evidence; the two callees' names suggest a choice between announcing
///   a changed game state and withdrawing the game's listing.
///
/// \param peer The peer connection (the SDK's PEER handle). Must not be
///             null.
///
/// \see PeerOperationsComplete, peerParseQuery

/// \fn int peerParseQuery(void *peer, char *query, int len, struct sockaddr *sender)
/// \brief GameSpy Peer SDK: passes one datagram received on the reporting
///        socket to the QR2 query parser.
///
/// While a game is hosted, its reporting socket receives GameSpy query
/// datagrams. checkQR2Queries polls that socket without blocking, reads
/// each datagram into a buffer, terminates it with a zero byte and passes
/// it here. In Zero Hour the peer thread calls checkQR2Queries; BFME 2's
/// checkQR2Queries has the same body as Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The connection can hold two query-reporting records. The first one that
/// is set receives the query through a tail jump to qr2_parse_queryA. When
/// neither is set, the datagram is ignored.
///
/// \param peer   The peer connection (the SDK's PEER handle).
/// \param query  The received datagram.
/// \param len    Its length in bytes.
/// \param sender The sender's address, passed through to the parser.
/// \return Nothing meaningful. The parser it forwards to, qr2_parse_queryA,
///         returns void, and its only caller, checkQR2Queries, ignores the
///         value in both BFME 2 and Zero Hour. The int return type in this
///         tree's source carries no meaning (this tree's sweep shim of the
///         Peer SDK header declares it as PEERBool).
///
/// \note On the path with no record the result register happens to hold 0,
///       the null pointer just tested; after the tail jump it holds
///       whatever the parser left there. A port should declare the
///       function void.
///
/// \see peerStopGame, PeerOperationsComplete

/// \fn void DisconnectManager::resetPlayerTimeout(Int slot)
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief Restarts one remote player's timeout by stamping the current
///        time.
///
/// Every remote player has a time stamp of the last moment the local
/// machine heard from it or reset it. DisconnectManager::hasPlayerTimedOut
/// reports a player as timed out once the time since that stamp reaches a
/// GameData timeout value, and the disconnect screen shows the time left.
/// Callers include DisconnectManager::resetPlayerTimeouts, which restarts
/// every remote player's timeout (in Zero Hour, each time a new frame is
/// reached and when the disconnect screen comes up), and BFME 2's
/// DisconnectManager::update, which restarts a player's timeout on each
/// update in which it does not count that player as stalled. In Zero
/// Hour a received disconnect keep-alive from a player also restarts that
/// player's timeout.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Stores timeGetTime in the stamp for \p slot. The same one-line body as
/// Zero Hour and BFME 1.
///
/// \param slot The player's index with the local player removed (see the
///             class description), not the raw player slot.
///
/// \warning There is no range check. Callers convert the slot first and
///          skip the local player, whose index is -1.
///
/// \see DisconnectManager::resetPlayerTimeouts,
///      DisconnectManager::hasPlayerTimedOut, DisconnectManager::update

/// \fn bool Connection::isQueueEmpty()
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief Reports whether this connection has no commands left to send or
///        to have acknowledged.
///
/// Its retail callers are ConnectionManager::areAllQueuesEmpty, which is
/// true when every existing connection's queue is empty (in Zero Hour it
/// also answers canILeave and gates the start of a file transfer), and
/// ConnectionManager::update, which deletes a quitting connection once its
/// quit frame has passed and this returns true.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// True when the connection's command list has no first entry. The same
/// body as Zero Hour's and BFME 1's.
///
/// \return true when the queue is empty.
///
/// \see Connection::setQuitting, ConnectionManager::areAllQueuesEmpty

/// \file GameNetwork/native_packet_crc.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief The rotate-and-add checksum that BFME 2's network code, LAN lobby,
///        quick-match request, firewall probe and logic random state share.
///
/// The file holds one free function, the rotate-and-add checksum. The name
/// the source gives it comes from a WorldBuilder function that is paired
/// with this routine by call graph only, an unconfirmed lead, so this
/// guide does not use it. Zero Hour computes the same per-byte step inline
/// with its CRC class (Common/crc.h), for example in Transport and in
/// GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC; in BFME 2 those callers call this free
/// function instead.
///
/// \par What it computes
/// The function takes a byte pointer, a byte count and a running value, and
/// returns the new running value. For each byte, the running value is
/// rotated left by one bit (the top bit comes back in at the bottom) and
/// the byte is added, modulo 2 to the 32nd power. Callers start a new
/// checksum with 0 and pass the previous result to continue over another
/// buffer. A null pointer or a zero count returns the running value
/// unchanged; beyond that null test the buffer is not checked. Although
/// the engine calls these values CRCs, this is not a polynomial CRC; it is
/// a simple additive checksum, enough to catch accidental damage but
/// trivial to forge. The game executable also contains a second copy that computes the same function with the
/// same per-byte loop but was compiled without a frame pointer.
///
/// \par Where it is used
/// Its callers in the game executable include:
/// - Transport::queueSend, which stores the checksum of each outgoing
///   message, and Transport::doRecv, which drops and counts as bad any
///   incoming message whose stored checksum does not match.
/// - GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC, which hashes the logic random state into
///   the game-state CRC, so a divergence in logic draws shows up as a CRC
///   mismatch (\ref sub_save_load_crc).
/// - The file checksum routine that reads a file in blocks and adds each
///   block (in Zero Hour, MapUtil.cpp's calcCRC, which gives the map cache
///   its per-map CRC).
/// - LANAPI::RequestHasMap, which checksums the map's portable path and
///   sends it with the local player's has-map flag, and
///   LANAPI::handleHasMap, which handles that message.
/// - PeerThreadClass::doQuickMatch, which sends the checksum of a 16-byte
///   hash in the quick-match request, and
///   FirewallHelperClass::getManglerResponse, which rejects a firewall
///   probe reply whose checksum does not match.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The value must stay bit-exact. A changed algorithm makes Transport drop
/// every packet from an unmodified client and makes LAN map checks
/// disagree, and because GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC feeds
/// GameLogic::getCRC, it changes the game-state CRC as well. In Zero Hour,
/// recorded replays are checked against stored CRCs during playback, so
/// there a changed algorithm would also make existing replays report
/// mismatches.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Keep the byte order of the inputs as well as the algorithm: callers
/// that pass integers, such as the logic random state, hash their
/// little-endian bytes.
///
/// \see GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC, Transport::queueSend, Transport::doRecv,
///      LANAPI::RequestHasMap

/// \file GameSpy/PeerOperationsComplete.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_network_lan_peer_crc
/// \brief The GameSpy Peer SDK's operation-finished test,
///        PeerOperationsComplete.
///
/// \see PeerOperationsComplete

